# Cloudflare Workers Assets reads this file (it ships in `public/`, so the build
# copies it to `.open-next/assets/` alongside sw.js and the manifest).
#
# Why it is needed at all: Workers Assets defaults every file to
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate`, which is the safe default for content whose URL
# can be rewritten and the wrong one for content whose URL cannot. Next's build
# output is the second kind. Without this, every chunk is revalidated on every
# load — and because OpenNext's ETags are not stable, revalidation is a full
# download rather than a 304.
#
# This is deliberately *not* done in next.config.ts. That file's rule excludes
# `/_next/` precisely because these paths are served by the assets binding
# before the Worker's router ever sees them, so a header set there would not
# arrive. Two mechanisms, one for documents and one for assets, and the comment
# in next.config.ts says which is which.

# Content-hashed by the build: a changed file is a changed filename, never a
# changed body at the same URL. This is the one case where a year is honest, and
# `immutable` additionally tells the browser not to revalidate even on reload.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The opposite case, stated rather than left to the default. A stale service
# worker outlives the page that registered it and keeps handling events with old
# code, so this one must always be checked. It is also the file whose staleness
# would be hardest to diagnose.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
